  "raw_text": "Step 1: Start by isolating the absolute value term. Add 10 to both sides of the equation to get rid of the constant on the left side.\nStep 2: This gives us $|x-4| = 12$.\n\nStep 3: The equation $|x-4| = 12$ means that $x-4$ can be either 12 or -12 because the absolute value of a number is its distance from zero on the number line, without regard to direction.\n\nStep 4: Set up two equations based on the definition of absolute value:\n   - For $x-4 = 12$, solve for $x$ to find one solution.\n   - For $x-4 = -12$, solve for $x$ to find the other solution.\n\nStep 5: Solve $x-4 = 12$ to get $x = 16$.\nStep 6: Solve $x-4 = -12$ to get $x = -8$.\n\nStep 7: The possible values of $x$ are 16 and -8. To find the product of these values, multiply them together.\n\nStep 8: Calculate the product: $16 \\times (-8) = -128$.\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{-128}<|im_end|>",
